#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0392894c-6bbc-544a-8ab5-c0d2be332967" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Missing Number in a Pascal's Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "4014e57c-3c50-5358-a9ae-280610aa6cfb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5107" :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $1$ and $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $1$ and $3\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = 1 + 3 = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f386de30-38b8-59b7-a6e8-ea5e386671d5" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "57c2e733-51b4-51f5-8774-ebbd6da2321d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5152" :question/problem "Expand the binomial $(2z + 1)^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n.\n$$\n\nWe have that $n=4,$ $a  = 2z,$ and $b= 1.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\na^4b^0,\\quad  a^{3}b^1,\\quad  a^{2}b^2,\\quad  a^1b^3,\\quad  a^0b^4.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t(2z + 1)^4   &=\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4\\[5pt]\n&=\\mathbf{1}(2z)^4(1)^0 + \\mathbf{4}(2z)^3(1)^1 + \\mathbf{6}(2z)^2(1)^2 + \\mathbf{4}(2z)^1(1)^3 + \\mathbf{1}(2z)^0(1)^4\\[5pt]\n\n&=(2z)^4 + 4(2z)^3 + 6(2z)^2 + 4(2z) +1\\[5pt]\n\n&=16z^4 + 32z^3 + 24z^2 + 8z + 1\n\n                       \n\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "bc866f37-17b6-59e0-9088-fd71a46c5b9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5158" :question/problem "Expand the binomial $(3x-y)^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a  = 3x,$ and $b= -y.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\na^4b^0,\\quad  a^{3}b^1,\\quad  a^{2}b^2,\\quad  a^1b^3,\\quad  a^0b^4.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t(3x - y)^4   \n&=\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4\\[5pt]\n&=\n\t\\mathbf{1}(3x)^4(-y)^0 + \\mathbf{4}(3x)^3(-y)^1 + \\mathbf{6}(3x)^2(-y)^2 + \\mathbf{4}(3x)^1(-y)^3 + \\mathbf{1}(3x)^0(-y)^4\\[5pt]\n                          &=\n\t\\mathbf{1}\\left(81x^4\\right)(1) + \\mathbf{4}\\left(27x^3\\right)(-y) + \\mathbf{6}\\left(9x^2\\right)(y^2) + \\mathbf{4}(3x)(-y^3) + \\mathbf{1}(1)(y^4) \\[5pt]\n                         &=  81x^4 - 108x^3y + 54x^2y^2 - 12xy^3 + y^4.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/id #uuid "b3546d6e-8399-5694-85db-c73f0a57b95c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5159" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of  ${(2x^2+y)}^3 .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=3,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n\\left(2x^2\\right)^3y^0, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\left(2x^2\\right)^2 y^1}},\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^1 y^2,\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^0 y^3.\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns $x^4y$ is the second. So, we are looking for the second term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,{\\color{red}3},3,1).$ Therefore, the second term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(2{x}^{2})}^{2}{(y)}^{1} & =3(4{x}^{4})(y) \\\\  & =12{x}^{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of ${(2x^2+y)}^3$ is $12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0392894c-6bbc-544a-8ab5-c0d2be332967" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Missing Number in a Pascal's Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a3b48b-b9d3-5daf-b3b0-f5f4f566089f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491470} {:answer/id #uuid "53f3072f-edd2-5544-9c6b-72edc6e7c1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491615} {:answer/id #uuid "5409ef18-5e70-5adb-addc-1ae48ed88a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491616} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d23774-6298-5410-9fe3-6ab519fa526b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491838} {:answer/id #uuid "e4561f9e-0bc2-5584-8ae3-7e0ee952d2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5409ef18-5e70-5adb-addc-1ae48ed88a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491616} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66ad25d-aec4-563a-8849-bb0750298a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f10ffe0-480f-5e92-a4dc-edbf0f536c4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110576" :question/problem "Express the missing number as the sum of two numbers from Pascal's triangle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & ◻ &  & 35 &  & 35 &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $6$ and $15.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & ◻ &  & 35 &  & 35 &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $6$ and $15.$ \n\n$$\n◻=6+15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0392894c-6bbc-544a-8ab5-c0d2be332967" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Missing Number in a Pascal's Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c3312b2-946c-52e6-9c9f-af514b378a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491512} {:answer/id #uuid "29ec2521-1737-586e-9cd8-2b63fd0d9df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491540} {:answer/id #uuid "3faba281-fb11-5e0f-b9cd-5d4ab53cb80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491580} {:answer/id #uuid "bd450e1b-1c56-5803-8bf0-3d3fa6025dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491779} {:answer/id #uuid "d402dc28-4222-5370-b573-d4108f999d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c3312b2-946c-52e6-9c9f-af514b378a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491512} :answer-field/id #uuid "56439851-b1a9-5024-aa32-d6bde6a4795a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eda5d85b-de52-5d2f-abad-d370d89d2617" :question/math-academy-id "q-110580" :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & 21 &  & 35 &  & ◻ &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $20$ and $15.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & 21 &  & 35 &  & ◻ &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $20$ and $15.$ \n\n$$\n◻=20+15=35\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f386de30-38b8-59b7-a6e8-ea5e386671d5" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bf8b50-3f39-5a0e-a1f9-19cc26b4e8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491455} {:answer/id #uuid "07a0b4fd-3612-587d-b648-d556fa08fa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}+16{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491469} {:answer/id #uuid "32d9dbb4-f28d-504e-a247-585aad76ea3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+12{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491557} {:answer/id #uuid "bf864856-3bd9-553f-91fa-c9242f57bf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491784} {:answer/id #uuid "d754a224-720e-52be-a22a-203492daf179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}+32{t}^{3}+96{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf864856-3bd9-553f-91fa-c9242f57bf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491784} :answer-field/id #uuid "41287685-a6c0-5218-984a-5454e51a8b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb084350-7fbe-5022-a4f9-36da32efbecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110589" :question/problem "Find, in descending powers of $t,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(t+2)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=t,$ and $b=2.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t+2)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}{b}^{0}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(t)^{4}(2)^{0}+4(t)^{3}(2)^{1}+6(t)^{2}(2)^{2}+4(t)^{1}(2)^{3}+1(t)^{0}(2)^{4} \\\\  & =1({t}^{4})(1)+4({t}^{3})(2)+6({t}^{2})({2}^{2})+4(t)({2}^{3})+1({t}^{0})({2}^{4}) \\\\  & ={t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}+32t+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $t,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion are\n\n$$\n{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b6d424-6151-5260-8d1a-6a2900f6f79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491466} {:answer/id #uuid "0a4d370c-d16f-5c25-b9ab-d451642a1aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491475} {:answer/id #uuid "192cab35-768d-5181-8fec-591ea58412f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491503} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8bd7b4-4ec6-5fb7-b481-83d13b4b0b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491625} {:answer/id #uuid "adf4bf83-0cea-5c2a-b235-8ac4e1021977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06b6d424-6151-5260-8d1a-6a2900f6f79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491466} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e586cdb-56eb-504c-81fd-9a35ab291f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb756db8-08d4-574e-9721-71fce97c078b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110705" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${t}^{9}$ in the expansion of  ${(4-2{t}^{3})}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{({t}^{3})}^{0},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{1},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{2},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{3},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{4}\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${t}^{9}$ is the fourth. So, we are looking for the fourth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the fourth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({4}^{1}){(-2{t}^{3})}^{3} & =4(4)(-8{t}^{9})=-128{t}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${t}^{9}$ in the expansion of ${(4-2{t}^{3})}^{4}$ is $-128.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84be836c-a587-5a9f-92a5-4202ac33a7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491682} {:answer/id #uuid "a963df3d-8d44-5507-a2b7-f23e331ccbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491750} {:answer/id #uuid "d0599db5-4e2d-50b8-aca9-30e3737df7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491808} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c1149e-90ad-53e2-8abd-dc4cfebb4faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491822} {:answer/id #uuid "e5700f82-0652-5901-842f-672506c08567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0599db5-4e2d-50b8-aca9-30e3737df7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491808} :answer-field/id #uuid "4412a8b1-5a3b-5f0c-ab80-8e1bb5a30756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cdcec28-9eea-5d7b-863d-1e35767ec2c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110707" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the expansion of  $(2x-3)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{x}^{4},\\,{x}^{3},\\,{x}^{2},\\,{x}^{1},\\,{x}^{0}\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${x}^{2}$ is the third. So, we are looking for the third term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the third term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(2x)^{2}{(-3)}^{2} & =6(4{x}^{2})(9)=216{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the expansion of $(2x-3)^{4}$ is $216.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1096edf0-16af-5236-8173-4762378e1310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}z+15{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491488} {:answer/id #uuid "1aaf67c6-84a3-529f-bd3a-02f8521fb4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491505} {:answer/id #uuid "26b6a154-d7ce-5869-b7d3-8f755d415bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491533} {:answer/id #uuid "615ae422-8dba-5d0d-ab94-d66ebe11a101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491627} {:answer/id #uuid "634114ad-6c02-51f7-b56e-9745a18ff622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+10{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aaf67c6-84a3-529f-bd3a-02f8521fb4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491505} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec8b379-7fc5-5e9a-a35d-30cbe0d09a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3050d934-f604-5f69-a586-6c218326013a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12484" :question/problem "Find, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(y+z)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=y,$ and $b=z.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(y)^{4}(z)^{0}+4(y)^{3}(z)^{1}+6(y)^{2}(z)^{2}+4(y)^{1}(z)^{3}+1(y)^{0}(z)^{4} \\\\  & =1({y}^{4})(1)+4({y}^{3})(z)+6({y}^{2})({z}^{2})+4(y)({z}^{3})+1(1)({z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+4y{z}^{3}+{z}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "328b96bc-3327-5e07-b15f-09c688c72d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491555} {:answer/id #uuid "61cc7245-8546-56f3-82b8-2f0c2bf433bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491631} {:answer/id #uuid "6edfe013-3073-56cc-bc3b-310a4cee6524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491654} {:answer/id #uuid "82d06102-2f93-5516-8d74-5c64ca9e9586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491680} {:answer/id #uuid "c14257ed-a13e-50f0-ad3d-677e3731752d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82d06102-2f93-5516-8d74-5c64ca9e9586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491680} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df69f96-84ad-5e71-9c31-65c276575d99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1561558e-21f5-576d-9b7c-91bcf7619901" :question/math-academy-id "q-12488" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${s}^{5}$ in the expansion of  $(3+2s)^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=5,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{s}^{0},\\,{s}^{1},\\,{s}^{2},\\,{s}^{3},\\,{s}^{4},\\,{s}^{5}.\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${s}^{5}$ is the fifth. So, we are looking for the fifth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore, the fifth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1(3)^{0}(2s)^{5} & =32{s}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${s}^{5}$ in the expansion of $(3+2s)^{5}$ is $32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f8f548-1a49-5e89-92e8-7aa6e15f79f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098038} {:answer/id #uuid "b7101996-4e35-5131-bd5e-49935795be88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098039} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5d0997-0855-5ead-8f10-8018708134ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098040} {:answer/id #uuid "e3de5239-b152-58fc-a20d-335a98123085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098041} {:answer/id #uuid "1a11a0cb-3075-5071-a3f1-76be246ed14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a11a0cb-3075-5071-a3f1-76be246ed14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098042} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef447e9-f097-5387-9108-98c2445f35bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfb7c79b-ea9c-5c43-a6d6-7b5de5669f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12875" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{(y+{x}^{2})}^{3}={y}^{3}+A{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+By{x}^{4}+C{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=y,$ and $b={x}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+{x}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(y)^{3}{({x}^{2})}^{0}+3(y)^{2}{({x}^{2})}^{1}+3(y)^{1}{({x}^{2})}^{2}+1(y)^{0}{({x}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1{y}^{3}(1)+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+3y{x}^{4}+1(1){x}^{6} \\\\  & ={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+3y{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3,B=3,$ and $C=1.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=3+3+1=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab7dbdf-cdeb-5d59-b993-8e3c334667e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+12y{z}^{2}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491567} {:answer/id #uuid "7f51ea30-b85b-5aaa-8db2-5c054cbf097c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{24{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491677} {:answer/id #uuid "9c65c1ea-1534-5429-9fa5-eb19d6c92c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491724} {:answer/id #uuid "f225180b-2833-5feb-9ee4-590d742c0ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+12y{z}^{2}-\\frac{24{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491868} {:answer/id #uuid "f70054c3-f419-516b-9432-262356961613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f70054c3-f419-516b-9432-262356961613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491876} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dfe3b1d-64a2-5aa6-947a-b91c6e82fc88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac49f7f6-9f77-5cda-ac3b-cdee9bcbb73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12892" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}},$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{3}{(-{z}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{2}{(-{z}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{1}{(-{z}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{0}{(-{z}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}})(1)+3(\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{z}^{4}})(-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})({z}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}+\\frac{6y{z}^{4}}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+6y{z}^{2}-{z}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}=-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058905e9-4d11-5574-a85c-d2d747756cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491460} {:answer/id #uuid "15d39e02-a5f2-5bb5-b092-37464400d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491498} {:answer/id #uuid "42ee8705-56f2-5f77-8daf-070e169b799d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491583} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd18569-c173-59a1-bf20-338f78ed324f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491597} {:answer/id #uuid "df61de6e-e6d3-5895-9840-de4d3b298884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df61de6e-e6d3-5895-9840-de4d3b298884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491833} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b878df-ae21-5b04-a919-8ba7f8eb6a11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab2cb57c-ba4d-5126-ab49-971b151088ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-3744" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{(r+m)}^{3}=A{r}^{3}+B{r}^{2}m+Cr{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=r,$ and $b=m.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r+m)^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(r)^{3}(m)^{0}+3(r)^{2}(m)^{1}+3(r)^{1}(m)^{2}+1(r)^{0}(m)^{3} \\\\  & =1({r}^{3})(1)+3({r}^{2})(m)+3(r)({m}^{2})+1(1)({m}^{3}) \\\\  & ={r}^{3}+3{r}^{2}m+3r{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=1,B=3,$ and $C=3.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=1+3+3=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f386de30-38b8-59b7-a6e8-ea5e386671d5" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2283d911-bad8-5d7c-a1c0-284b5c6924b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-160n+300{n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491525} {:answer/id #uuid "31c931e7-744c-5a82-b705-86dc1a635969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-160n+600{n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491552} {:answer/id #uuid "55179f7d-8d07-574b-80ee-6436c8610ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-80n+300{n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491617} {:answer/id #uuid "65a8aad5-c264-545f-a300-db72f7739c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-160n+350{n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491638} {:answer/id #uuid "c441fca8-b3a3-51b0-9e25-d8495cda4c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-80n+600{n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31c931e7-744c-5a82-b705-86dc1a635969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-160n+600{n}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491552} :answer-field/id #uuid "00fe569c-7704-57d0-bfbe-6b9477f1dab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43307ab9-268b-5315-bfa7-ff0a17a7f84a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3751" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $n,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2-5n)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2,$ and $b=-5n.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5n)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(2)^{4}(-5n)^{0}+4(2)^{3}(-5n)^{1}+6(2)^{2}(-5n)^{2}+4(2)(-5n)^{3}+1(2)^{0}(-5n)^{4} \\\\  & =1(16)(1)+4(8)(-5n)+6(4)(25{n}^{2})+4(2)(-125{n}^{3})+1(1)(625{n}^{4}) \\\\  & =16-160n+600{n}^{2}-1\\,000{n}^{3}+625{n}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $n$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n16-160n+600{n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc8e8c3-2539-5d2e-b249-962fdc897a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491477} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa04281-3db0-59dc-8d17-70ecc80f8121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491504} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa4b33e-bd16-5df7-a8d1-f47a14078132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491541} {:answer/id #uuid "493bcceb-1e87-5ee7-8c50-0ef2a21d1f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491593} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0aaff4-ceb1-5c9b-82e1-bd1f4b766029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cc8e8c3-2539-5d2e-b249-962fdc897a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491477} :answer-field/id #uuid "8197bfc5-0ad1-5f9c-bf1d-1334bb43fb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "583748e6-2c8b-5634-bc36-5084c91f8d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73162" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{4}$ in the expansion of  ${(z+2x)}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{z}^{4}{x}^{0},\\,{z}^{3}{x}^{1},\\,{z}^{2}{x}^{2},\\,{z}^{1}{x}^{3},\\,{z}^{0}{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${x}^{4}$ is the fifth. So, we are looking for the fifth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the fifth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1{(z)}^{0}{(2x)}^{4}=1(1)(16{x}^{4})=16{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{4}$ in the expansion of ${(z+2x)}^{4}$ is $16.$"}]}
